errors), and `git diff --stat` confirmed the isolated commit touches only
the two generator/course files.

## Consultation 5 — before committing Lesson 5 (df-l1-tangent / df-tangent-line, the final pair)

Question put to the advisor: verify the tangent-line maths (m = 2a·at+b,
height = a·at²+b·at+c, k = height−m·at) and the sampling guard
(2a·at+b != 0, never a horizontal tangent) independently rather than by
re-deriving my own reasoning; check the three choice distractors — "forgot
to shift", "substituted before differentiating", "used height as
gradient" — for whether they are genuinely wrong at every draw and whether
the filter dropping a candidate equal to the correct answer is live code
or dead code; explicitly flagged that this generator declares no `source`
(a tangent line is not a derivative of anything, so the oracle test has
nothing to check here by design) and asked the advisor to weigh that when
deciding how hard to look, since self-consistency is the only safety net;
asked it to hunt for the elision-bug class the last three reviews each
found once, while noting my own claim that this generator builds every
displayed line through `lineTex`/`sumTex`/`termTex` rather than hand-written
templates and asking it to verify that claim rather than trust it; checked
the lesson prose for the same "overclaims a general rule"/"leaks the
sampling policy" pattern; asked it to confirm the figure (`graph()` call,
the first of the four new lessons to use one) matches a shipped pattern
rather than assume it from a visual copy. Passed `model: "opus"` again.

Summary of the advisor's answer: safe to commit and push, one required
prose fix (~2 minutes), everything else a note for the final report, no
correctness bug.
- Verified the maths and every distractor **exhaustively** rather than by
  sampling: enumerated all 12,026 `(a,b,c,at)` tuples reachable across both
  difficulties (not a seed sweep), and for each independently checked
  tangency via `f(x) - line(x) === a(x-at)^2` at multiple probe points (a
  repeated root at `at` is the definition of a tangent) plus a
  central-difference derivative check — a strictly independent check, not
  a re-run of the generator's own formula, and the closest thing available
  to an oracle test for a generator that cannot have one. Zero failures.
- Confirmed my three claims about the distractors precisely, with counts
  rather than "yes/no": `(m, height)` never collides with the correct
  answer (0/12,026); `(0, height)` never collides (0/12,026); `(height,
  height - height*at)` collides in exactly 336/12,026 draws (2.8%) —
  confirming the `.filter()` is live code doing real work, not dead code,
  and adding a precision I'd missed: the collision needs *both* `m != 0`
  (the sampling guard) and `at != 0` (a separate `nonZero` call), so if
  `at` were ever relaxed to allow 0 that distractor would silently become
  correct. Also found, unprompted, that two of the three candidates can
  coincide *with each other* (not with the correct answer) in 322/12,026
  draws (2.7%, exactly when the curve's height at `at` is 0), which
  `options()` already dedups harmlessly since the two collision sets are
  disjoint — confirmed the slide never drops below 3 options across the
  whole space (4 in 11,368 draws, 3 in 658).
- **Found my "no hand-written templates" claim was false**, the fourth
  time in five rounds the advisor has found something in this exact bug
  class: solution step 4 (`y - ${height} = ${m}\left(x - ${at}\right)`) is
  a raw template, not routed through `lineTex`, so it can show a bare
  `1(...)` coefficient or a double sign like `y - -7 = -1\left(x - -2\right)`
  (measured: 63% of sampled draws show a double sign somewhere in that
  line; the bare-1 case hits ~11% of difficulty-1 draws). But it then did
  something the earlier rounds didn't: checked whether this is a
  regression by sweeping every *other* registered generator's solution TeX
  for the same `+ -N`/`- -N` pattern, and found it is a long-standing
  codebase-wide idiom (8 existing generators show it, `evaluate-derivative`
  — in this same level and level check — worse than the new one), not
  something this generator introduced. Classified as a known limitation to
  record, not a blocker for this commit, and named a possible future fix
  (a shared `signedTex` helper in `calculus.ts`, since `quadratics.ts` and
  `integration.ts` each already have their own private version of the same
  helper) explicitly as a separate refactor outside this session's scope.
- Found the one required fix: the T3 teach slide and the solution's final
  step both stated "substituting before differentiating gives a horizontal
  line, [which misses the curve / is not the tangent]" as an unqualified
  rule. That's only true because the generator's own sampling excludes
  `m = 0` by construction — a quadratic's tangent at its actual turning
  point genuinely is horizontal, so the claim is false in general and
  contradicts a slide two lessons earlier (`df-l1-sums`) that correctly
  teaches "a derivative of zero means the curve is momentarily flat, which
  is how stationary points are found." Worse, it hands the learner an
  exploit: the "substituted before differentiating" distractor always
  renders as a bare constant with no `x` in it, so a learner who has just
  been taught "horizontal is always wrong here" can eliminate that option
  on sight rather than by understanding it, cutting the effective guess
  rate on `+choice` slides. Fixed both the T3 prose (reworded to "a
  horizontal line, which is only ever the actual tangent at a turning
  point") and the solution's final step (made draw-specific — "$0$ rather
  than the $m$ found above" — instead of a blanket claim).
- Confirmed several things without needing a fix: the `graph()` figure
  call is structurally identical to the already-shipped `df-l1-sums`
  tangent figure and checked the mark/curve geometry by hand against the
  280x150 viewBox to confirm nothing clips; agreed the T2 worked example
  sitting inside the generator's own difficulty-1 sample space is the same
  low-priority, pre-existing, codebase-wide pattern already accepted for
  lessons 1 and 3 and should stay unfixed for consistency; confirmed
  lesson structure (10 guided slides matching `df-l1-index`'s own
  teach/3/teach/2/teach/2 shape, 3 skill-check, three widget shapes
  including `df-evaluate-steps`'s `reduce` kind) and the completed
  15-entry `df-l1` level check are all correct; confirmed no file outside
  the two generator/course files changed.
- Flagged several things explicitly for this final report rather than as
  fixes: no committed test would catch a future edit breaking the
  `k = height - m*at` line, since there is no oracle for this generator —
  a candidate follow-up would be a generator-specific test asserting
  `f(at) = line(at)` and `f'(at) = m` symbolically; the `m = 0` exclusion
  is enforced only by the sampling guard and by convention in the prose,
  with nothing coupling the two if either is edited later; a stale comment
